e  what i've done is something called a  superposition  superposition is a fancy word that means  i added them together  so i'm going to stick together the two  states we had before  in such a way that the wave is wiggling  more on the couch  than it is on the tree this is a thought  experiment so we can make  anything we like let's say we make it  seventy percent on the couch and  thirty percent in the tree  waves can do that there's no problem  with that given its wave properties a  quantum mechanical particle has  no problem wiggling its wave a little  bit over here  and also wiggling it a little bit over  there  so right now where is the particle  there's still just one particle that's  very important  but its wave now has two wiggly parts  it really is in two places at once  so this is how you could sit in three  chairs at once and solve the problem of  the date at the movie theater  if you were at a quantum movie theater  rather than getting into an argument  about where to sit or just sitting where  neither of you wants to sit  you could both make your waves wiggle a  little bit in the front row  wiggle a little bit in the middle row  and wiggle a little bit in the back row  problem solved you'd simultaneously be  sitting  in three rows at once  but let's return to the quantum living  room when we last saw  our quantum particle it was sitting in a  mixed state  its wave was wiggling a little bit in  the tree and  a little bit on the couch we set this  wave up so that it's  70 percent on the couch and 30 percent  in the tree  and this is completely fine for a wave  to do  but then let's think about what happens  if we try to measure the particle's  position  when it's in this mixed state this is  like asking the particle  where it is and according to the laws of  quantum physics  only certain answers are allowed it's as  though  every time we make a measurement the  particle is forced  to fill out a quantum measurement form  with only  certain discrete check boxes when we ask  it where it is  the particle is not allowed to say i'm  part way between the couch and the tree  because it is not somewhere in the  middle rather  it's in both places at once when we make  this position measurement  the particle must return some answer  and that answer has to correspond to a  pure state of the question  that we asked in the case of this mixed  state  couch and tree particle only couch or  tree  are allowable answers this  is the crux of measurements in the  quantum world  since the answer must be either tree  or couch and it can't be somewhere in  between  we say the answers are quantized in  other words  when we ask this mixed particle where it  is  there's a 30 percent chance it will say  tree and there's a 70  chance it will say couch and no one has  any control  over which answer it will give it turns  out that the particle itself  doesn't even know which answer it will  give  and we the askers have no way of  determining  in advance which answer the particle  will give  the limitation here is not just a  limitation of our experiments or of our  measurements  the particle itself doesn't know what  it's going to say  until it has already become that answer  to my mind this is by far the weirdest  of all quantum phenomena  and it's weird enough that i'd like to  recap the situation  when a mixed state particle is forced by  our question to give us  an allowable answer it will suddenly  and uncontrollably be thrown into a pure  state of the type  the question demands when we ask the  question  we force the particle into a pure state  of that question type  but we have no way of knowing which  state that's going to be  then when the particle gives its answer  it becomes a pure state  and it will stay that way until  otherwise disturbed  in quantum language this sudden and  uncontrollable  snapping into a new pure state when a  measurement happens  that's what's referred to as wave  function collapse  now you might be asking yourself the  extremely important question  how do you know that wave function  collapse is really what's happening  the answer is we don't remember we can't  measure the wave function directly so we  can't  directly track it during these  measurements  however what we can say is that the  answers that come  out of the measurements that we take in  a laboratory  match the assumption of wave function  collapse  and that's why we continue to describe  what's called  orthodox quantum theory using that  assumption  or sometimes you'll hear it called a  postulate  but this is a piece of quantum theory  that could very well get  upgraded as we perform new experiments  experiments which  push the boundaries of our current  knowledge on this are being undertaken  by scientists around the world  for now i'll incorporate the assumption  of wave function collapse  into our discussions in this course  because it correctly  describes the results of all of the  experiments that have been done  so far so now back to our quantum living  room  where we just took a measurement on that  particle that was  in the mixed state the act of taking the  measurement  forced the particle to suddenly and  uncontrollably be thrown into a pure  state  of the type of measurement we were  taking as a direct result of our  measurement process  the particle suddenly snapped into a new  state so we see that a  big important principle in quantum  mechanics is that  observers disturb what they measure  let me give you a good analogy for just  how disturbing these measurements can be  imagine somebody trying to measure the  position of a crystal chandelier  blindfolded with a baseball bat  i'm willing to bet that you probably  wouldn't want someone walking into your  home  and figuring out where your crystal  chandelier is by blindfolding themselves  and swinging a baseball bat around  but it would work wouldn't it they could  use that method to find out the location  of the chandelier on the other hand  they would change the system in the  process  what we're encountering here is the  phenomenon that observers  disturb what they measure this issue  actually comes up in  every branch of science observers always  disturb what they measure  in science every time we make a  measurement in any context  we're always disturbing the system on  the scales that you're used to  it's a very weak disturbance for example  this video  is a type of measurement about what's  happening in the studio right now  does this measurement make disturbances  you bet it does  how does the camera capture its series  of images to make a video  the camera can capture images only when  the lights are on  photons are coming out of the light  sources then those photons are bouncing  off of me  and then heading back toward the camera  now for the photons to bounce off of me  they have to interact with me when the  photons hit me  they have to make some sort of  disturbance in order to get turned  around  and headed back toward the camera so  every single time we make any  measurement at all  we must disturb the system we're  measuring  we're used to cases where the  disturbance is minimal like the tiny  photons bouncing off of a large  macroscopic object but in quantum  mechanics the disturbance  is going to be a huge effect  let's return to our quantum living room  and find out the results of having taken  a position measurement on the mixed  state remember that when taking a  quantum measurement it's like the  particle has to fill out the results on  some quantum measurement form  and there are little check boxes and  only certain  answers are allowed other answers are  simply  forbidden let's say that when we asked  the particle where it is  it replied and snapped into the couch  state  that of course is a pure position state  not a mixture of two position states and  it is true although it's not the whole  truth  once we make the measurement and force  the particle to give up its mixed  state wave and become a pure state  after that we have no way of finding out  that it was ever in the mixed state  before  that information has been completely  lost  but from now on if we ask the particle  again where it  is right away the particle will say it's  on the couch  do you remember the movie theater you  really could sit in three chairs at once  that is until someone asked you which  chair you're sitting in  at that point you would be suddenly and  uncontrollably thrown  into one and only one seat and  all memory of your prior mixed state  would be erased a spinning coin is a  good analogy for the way in which these  measurements  force particular answers to come out and  for the fact that we don't have control  over which answer comes  up here's my quantum coin and just like  a regular coin it has  heads on one side and tails on the other  something that often comes up when you  make measurements of the quantum world  is that you'll end up asking questions  that aren't quite well defined until you  go to ask the question  so let's say for example i do this  [Music]  it came up tails that time and  i can go on measuring let's see what  happens if i flip the coin again  this time it came up heads i could keep  on measuring like this  this type of measurement is unambiguous  right i'm just  flipping a coin and then i take a  measurement by looking to see which side  is  up there's nothing strange going on here  but  what if i instead do this and i spin the  coin  is it heads or tails now while it's  spinning  while it's in the act of spinning it's  neither heads nor tails  but think of it like this a wave can do  whatever it wants to do it can go into a  state that's like being neither heads  nor tails now to answer the question  that i want to ask  in order to make a measurement that's  going to give me back  heads or tails i'm going to have to  disturb the spinning coin  system is it heads or tails  it's tails now notice that in this case  i have to  fundamentally change what's going on in  order for me to answer the question  is it heads or tails in this case the  question  was not well defined until i actually  asked the question  many measurements in quantum mechanics  end up being a little bit like this  we have to change the system in order to  get back  some allowable answer corresponding to  the type of question we  asked or more precisely what type of  measurement we took  and so that's frustrating this sort of  thing  makes us really uncomfortable in quantum  mechanics  nobody likes this it's doing one thing  and then the active measurement forces  it to do something  different it's heads by the way  but we can at least get an answer back  this is a good analogy for a lot of what  happens in quantum mechanical  measurements this spinning coin is also  a good analogy  for how probabilities can present  themselves in the quantum world  if i asked you in advance while the coin  is spinning  what percentage chance do you think i  would have of measuring heads if it's in  a really good spin  you'd say 50 right and you just knew  that  you didn't have to do any fancy  mathematics or detailed calculations to  get that  a lot of quantum measurements are like  that we may not be able to predict the  exact  outcome but we will be able to predict  how often particular answers  come up everything we've said about  quantum measurements so far  leads us to another big idea in quantum  physics and  perhaps you've already heard the phrase  that describes it the phrase is this  observer determines reality in fact  if you've done a google search on  anything quantum recently  chances are you then got a lot of ads  about how you can use quantum mechanics  to create your own reality  now this phrase observer determines  reality it actually does appear  in the literature on quantum mechanics  but it's much more  limited than it may sound at first in  fact  observer determined reality in our  example of the spinning coin  while the coin is spinning it's neither  heads nor tails  then in the act of making the  measurement to find out  whether it's heads or tails we forced  the coin into  a new state and it became either heads  or tails  this is the sense in which we the  observers  determined the reality for the coin but  it's probably much more accurate to say  that the observer  affected reality something similar  happened in our example of  sitting in three chairs at once in the  quantum movie theater  at first you really were in three places  at once  and that was all fine and well and good  until an observer walked up and  asked you which seat you were sitting in  at that point  you were thrown into only one chair  that's the sense in which your observer  determined your reality  but again it's probably more accurate to  say that the observer  affected your reality you certainly  couldn't use this to  create your own reality  that's because no one had control over  which seat you ultimately  snapped into you didn't control the  outcome  from your perspective it was like a roll  of the dice and  the observer or the person who asked you  the question  certainly didn't control the outcome  when asked which seat you were in  it's as though there had been a roll of  the dice and the outcome of that  roll decided which seat your wave would  now  snap into well  i hope you've enjoyed exploring some of  the fascinating ideas in quantum physics  with me today  if you are finding some of these ideas  to be a little  well odd i want you to know that you're  in good company at first  even einstein refused to believe that  probabilities or percent chances  underlie quantum mechanics until the  evidence convinced him otherwise  the kinds of strange physical phenomena  we encounter at the quantum level  take a while to get used to but i like  to keep in mind  michael faraday's famous words nothing  is too wonderful to be true  in the next lecture we'll delve more  into the consequences of these  probabilities  and we'll explore what schrodinger's cat  can teach us about taking measurements  i'll see you then  [Music]  you
 

